<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-event-trajectory-model">
  <title>Event and Trajectory Model</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The previous chapter modeled a single <xref ref="gt-board" text="custom">board</xref>
      state. A <xref ref="gt-configuration" text="custom">configuration</xref> tells
      where every <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">card</xref> is at one moment,
      and a <xref ref="gt-membership-matrix" text="custom">membership matrix</xref>
      gives that state an algebraic representation. This chapter adds time. A
      student <xref ref="gt-attempt" text="custom">attempt</xref> is not one
      configuration; it is a logged sequence of events that move, mark, reset,
      or delimit the current state.
    </p>

    <p>
      The central object is an <term>event trajectory</term>. It records the
      states a student passes through, the events that produced or marked those
      states, and the times at which they occurred. Moves change the state.
      Checks mark the current state and return
      <xref ref="gt-feedback" text="title"/>. Resets jump back to the
      <xref ref="gt-initial-state" text="title"/>. Page events do not change the
      board, but they help delimit sessions and problem access. Timestamps turn
      successive events into gaps and other timing quantities that later chapters
      summarize.
    </p>

    <p>
      The state model answers <q>Where are the cards?</q> The event and
      <xref ref="gt-trajectory" text="title"/> model answers <q>How did that
      state change over time?</q> Later feature chapters use the trajectory
      defined here to measure progress curves, check cadence, excess moves,
      <xref ref="gt-takeback" text="custom">takebacks</xref>,
      <xref ref="gt-reset" text="custom">resets</xref>, and
      feedback-sensitive summaries.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-actions-and-event-logs">
    <title>Actions and Event Logs</title>

    <p>
      A logged drag-and-drop record is an ordered list of events. Each event has
      a time, an <xref ref="gt-action" text="custom">action</xref> type, and an
      action payload. The payload is what the platform recorded for that event:
      which card was moved, which state was checked, whether the board was
      reset, or whether the page was viewed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let
      <md>
        E_1,E_2,\dots,E_N
      </md>
      be the ordered raw event log for one student working on one item. We write
      a typical raw event as
      <md xml:id="an-event-record">
        E_\ell=(t_\ell,\tau_\ell,p_\ell),
      </md>
      where <m>t_\ell</m> is the timestamp, <m>\tau_\ell</m> is the recorded
      event type, and <m>p_\ell</m> is the recorded payload. The separate index
      <m>\ell</m> is used here because the raw event log and the reconstructed
      trajectory need not have exactly the same indexing after page delimiters,
      no-op drops, or malformed rows are handled by the processing pipeline.
    </p>

    <p>
      For <xref ref="gt-cardsort" text="custom">cardsort</xref> data, the main
      action types are:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>move</term>: one card is dropped into a destination
          <xref ref="gt-zone" text="custom">zone</xref>;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>check</term>: the current configuration is submitted to the
          grader and feedback is returned;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>reset</term>: the board is returned to the initial
          <xref ref="gt-tray" text="custom">tray</xref> state;
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>page</term>: the student opens or views the page containing the
          item.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      A <xref ref="gt-move" text="custom">move</xref> changes the board state. A
      <xref ref="gt-check" text="custom">check</xref> marks the current state
      without changing it. A reset returns the board to the initial state, unless
      the board is already there. Page events are not board actions; they help
      delimit <xref ref="gt-session" text="title"/> and problem access.
    </p>

    <p>
      These action types are the row types described in <xref ref="ch-dnd-anatomy"/>:
      the <c>Selection</c> column identifies the type, and the <c>Action</c> and
      <c>Input</c> columns carry the payload, with <c>Input</c> the authoritative
      copy of a checked configuration (see <xref ref="tab-datashop-columns"/>).
    </p>

    <p>
      The goal of the trajectory model is to convert the raw event log into a
      sequence of state records
      <md>
        X_0,X_1,\dots,X_n,
      </md>
      together with action marks and timestamps. Some events change the state,
      some repeat the current state with a new mark, and some delimit the trace
      without changing the board. The next sections fix these effects and then
      assemble them into trajectories.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-moves-as-state-transitions">
    <title>Moves as State Transitions</title>

    <p>
      A move is the elementary state transition. It changes the zone of one card
      and leaves every other card fixed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose that immediately before a move, card <m>j</m> is in zone <m>i</m>.
      The student drops the card into zone <m>h</m>, where <m>h\neq i</m>. In the
      <xref ref="gt-configuration-function" text="custom">configuration function</xref>,
      the new state <m>s'</m> agrees with <m>s</m> except at card <m>j</m>, where
      <m>s'(j)=h</m>. Thus a move changes exactly one coordinate of the
      <xref ref="gt-configuration-vector" text="custom">configuration vector</xref>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We record the move by the triple
      <md xml:id="an-move-triple">
        m=(j,i,h),
        \qquad
        j\in\mathcal{P},
        \quad
        i,h\in\mathcal{Z},
        \quad
        h\neq i.
      </md>
      The first entry identifies the card, the second its source zone, and the
      third its destination zone.
    </p>

    <p>
      In matrix form, the move replaces column <m>j</m> by <m>e_h</m> instead
      of <m>e_i</m>. The state model already introduced
      <m>e_0,e_1,\dots,e_R</m> as the standard basis vectors for zone rows. Let
      <m>f_1,f_2,\dots,f_P</m> denote the standard basis vectors of
      <m>\mathbb{R}^P</m>, indexed by cards. Then the move update is
      <md xml:id="an-move-state-update-matrix">
        X'
        =
        X+\Delta_m,
        \qquad
        \Delta_m
        =
        (e_h-e_i)f_j^{\top}.
      </md>
      The update matrix <m>\Delta_m</m> has a <m>-1</m> in row <m>i</m>, column
      <m>j</m>, and a <m>+1</m> in row <m>h</m>, column <m>j</m>. Separating the
      move <m>m</m> from its update matrix is useful when the moves of a
      trajectory are indexed as <m>m_1,m_2,\dots</m>: for a move step,
      <m>X_k-X_{k-1}=\Delta_{m_k}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The update preserves validity. Since
      <md>
        \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}(e_h-e_i)=0,
      </md>
      it follows that
      <md>
        \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}X'
        =
        \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}X
        +
        \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}(e_h-e_i)f_j^{\top}
        =
        \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}X.
      </md>
      Therefore a move carries one valid membership matrix to another.
    </p>

    <p>
      A <term>takeback</term> is a move whose destination is the tray. If card
      <m>j</m> currently occupies zone <m>i\neq 0</m>, then the takeback is
      <m>m=(j,i,0)</m>, with update
      <m>X'=X+(e_0-e_i)f_j^{\top}</m>. Algebraically, a takeback is an ordinary
      move. Analytically, it is worth naming because returning a card to the tray
      often marks a reversal, repair, or response to feedback, and later features
      count takebacks separately.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-configuration-graph">
    <title>The Configuration Graph</title>

    <p>
      Moves give the <xref ref="gt-configuration-space" text="custom">configuration
      space</xref> a graph structure. This structure makes the
      <xref ref="gt-distance" text="custom">distance</xref> of
      <xref ref="sec-distance"/> operational: a count of differing coordinates
      becomes a count of required single-card moves.
    </p>

    <p>
      The vertices of the <term>configuration graph</term> are the configurations
      in
      <md>
        \mathcal{C}=\mathcal{Z}^{\mathcal{P}}.
      </md>
      Two configurations are adjacent when they differ in exactly one coordinate:
      <md xml:id="an-configuration-graph-adjacency">
        \#\{j\in\mathcal{P}:s(j)\neq r(j)\}=1.
      </md>
      Each edge corresponds to a single move.
    </p>

    <p>
      This graph is the <term>Hamming graph</term>
      <md xml:id="an-configuration-graph">
        H(P,R+1).
      </md>
      Equivalently, one card's possible zones form a complete graph on
      <m>R+1</m> vertices, and the full graph is the Cartesian product
      <m>K_{R+1}^{\square P}</m>, one complete-graph factor per card. Every
      vertex has degree <m>P\cdot R</m>, because any of the <m>P</m> cards may
      move to any of the <m>R</m> zones it does not currently occupy.
    </p>

    <p>
      The graph distance in <m>H(P,R+1)</m> is exactly the Hamming distance
      <m>d_{\mathcal{C}}</m> from
      <xref ref="an-state-distance-function" text="custom">state distance</xref>:
      the shortest path between two configurations changes precisely the cards
      on which they differ. In particular, <m>d_{\mathcal{C}}(s,s^\star)</m> is
      the minimum number of single-card moves needed to solve the item from
      state <m>s</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      An observed move sequence is a walk through this graph, and a walk may be
      longer than the distance between its endpoints. The difference between the
      number of moves made and the graph distance covered motivates the later
      excess-move features. The graph itself describes possible transitions only;
      it does not say which transitions are likely, meaningful, or accessible to
      students.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-checks-as-marked-states">
    <title>Checks on States</title>

    <p>
      A check does not move a card. It marks the current configuration as a state
      submitted to the grader and attaches feedback determined by the
      <xref ref="gt-feedback-policy" text="custom">feedback policy</xref>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>\phi</m> be the feedback policy for the item, as defined at the
      design level. If the current state is <m>X=X(s)</m>, then pressing
      <q>check</q> leaves the board state unchanged:
      <md xml:id="an-check-state-update">
        X'=X.
      </md>
      What changes is the event mark: the state has now been submitted, and the
      platform records feedback such as Correct, Incorrect, a count, a refusal,
      or highlighted cards, depending on <m>\phi</m> and the check history.
    </p>

    <p>
      If a trajectory contains indexed states
      <md>
        X_0,X_1,\dots,X_n,
      </md>
      let
      <md xml:id="an-check-index-set">
        K=\{k_1,k_2,\dots,k_q\}\subseteq\{0,1,\dots,n\}
      </md>
      be the set of indices at which checks occurred, listed in increasing
      order. The checked states are the marked subsequence
      <md>
        X_{k_1},X_{k_2},\dots,X_{k_q}.
      </md>
      These states provide the outcome layer of the attempt: they are the
      configurations submitted for grading.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the <xref ref="gt-raw-data" text="custom">raw data</xref>, a check row
      is especially valuable because it records the full checked configuration as
      JSON, with the tray implicit, as illustrated in <xref ref="ch-dnd-anatomy"/>.
      The reconstruction section below states the parsing rule precisely.
    </p>

    <p>
      Checks also affect the interpretation of later moves. If feedback is
      detailed, then a move after a check may plausibly respond to that feedback.
      If feedback is only Correct or Incorrect, the same move has a different
      evidentiary status. Feedback-sensitive trajectory features should therefore
      always be interpreted relative to <m>\phi</m>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-resets-as-distinguished-jumps">
    <title>Resets as Distinguished Jumps</title>

    <p>
      A reset returns the board to the initial state <m>X_0=X(s_0)</m> of
      <xref ref="an-initial-state-matrix" text="custom">the state model</xref>:
      <md xml:id="an-reset-state-update">
        X\mapsto X_0,
        \qquad
        s\mapsto s_0.
      </md>
      It can be viewed as the simultaneous takeback of every placed card.
    </p>

    <p>
      Unless the current state differs from <m>X_0</m> in only one card, a reset
      is not a single-card transition and is therefore not an edge of the
      configuration graph. It is better treated as a distinguished jump back to
      the initial vertex.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the event log, reset rows are recorded separately from move rows, and
      later features count resets separately from moves and takebacks. A takeback
      returns one card to the tray; a reset returns the entire board to the tray
      state in one logged action.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-attempts-and-segments">
    <title>Trajectories, Attempts, and Segments</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The preceding sections fixed the effect of each board action on the
        state. This section assembles those effects into the object the rest of
        the framework analyzes: the event trajectory, the attempt it represents,
        and the segments into which an attempt may be divided.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-event-trajectories">
      <title>Event Trajectories</title>

      <p>
        An <term>event trajectory</term> is an ordered sequence
        <md xml:id="an-event-trajectory">
          \Gamma
          =
          \bigl(
          (a_0,t_0,X_0),
          (a_1,t_1,X_1),
          \dots,
          (a_n,t_n,X_n)
          \bigr),
        </md>
        where <m>X_k</m> is the board state at trajectory index <m>k</m>,
        <m>t_k</m> is the event time, and <m>a_k</m> is the processed action mark.
        The mark records whether the event is a start, move, check, reset, or
        page delimiter; for a move it contains the move triple, and for a check
        it contains the returned feedback or refusal status.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each state follows from the previous one by the rule for its action mark:
        a move applies <m>\Delta_{m_k}</m>, a check repeats the current state and
        marks it, a reset returns to <m>X_0</m>, and a page event leaves the board
        unchanged while recording a delimiter. The initial entry uses the
        conventional mark <m>a_0=\text{start}</m>; <m>t_0</m> is the time at which
        the initial state is first observed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Forgetting the actions and times leaves the bare state sequence
        <m>X_0,X_1,\dots,X_n</m>: where the board was. The marks and timestamps
        record how the state changed, when checks occurred, what feedback was
        returned, and where resets or page events interrupted the path.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-time-and-gaps">
      <title>Time and Gaps</title>

      <p>
        The timestamps carry their own information. The <term>gap</term> before
        event <m>k</m> is
        <md xml:id="an-event-gap">
          \delta_k=t_k-t_{k-1},
          \qquad
          k=1,\dots,n.
        </md>
        Restricting to consecutive events of one type gives named timing
        quantities: a <term>move gap</term> is the elapsed time between
        consecutive moves, and a <term>check gap</term> is the elapsed time
        between consecutive checks. The time from the start of an attempt to its
        first check is the <term>time to first check</term>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Gaps are trajectory data, not interpretations. A long pause before a move
        may indicate deliberation, distraction, or a session break; which reading
        is warranted depends on the item, feedback policy, and session structure.
        The trajectory model records the gaps. Later chapters decide how to
        summarize and interpret them.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-attempts-and-segments">
      <title>Attempts and Segments</title>

      <p>
        An <term>attempt</term> is the trajectory produced by one student working
        on one item under a chosen boundary rule. In the simplest case, it begins
        at the initial state and ends at the final checked state. In real logs,
        an attempt may contain multiple checks, resets,
        <xref ref="gt-page-view" text="custom">page views</xref>, or session
        breaks, and it is often useful to divide it into segments:

        <dl width="medium">
          <li xml:id="li-check-segment">
            <title>check segment</title>
            <p>
              The part of a trajectory between two consecutive checks, or between
              the initial state and the first check; the unit for studying feedback
              use and post-check revision.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li xml:id="li-reset-segment">
            <title>reset segment</title>
            <p>
              The part of a trajectory between two consecutive resets, or between
              the initial state and the first reset; the unit for studying restarts.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li xml:id="li-session-segment">
            <title>session segment</title>
            <p>
              The part of a trajectory occurring within one logged session; the
              unit for studying work abandoned and resumed later.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        For the running shapes question, one possible trajectory is
        <md>
          <mrow>
            (0,0,0,0)
            \to
            (1,0,0,0)
            \amp
            \to
            (1,1,0,0)
            \to
            (1,1,2,0)
            \to
            (1,1,2,3)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp
            \to
            (1,1,2,3)
            \to
            (1,1,2,0)
            \to
            (1,1,2,0).
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Steps <m>1</m> through <m>4</m> are moves. Step <m>5</m> is a check, so
        the configuration is repeated but marked as submitted to the grader and
        graded Incorrect. Step <m>6</m> is a takeback, returning the circle to the
        tray. Step <m>7</m> is another check, marking the current state Correct.
        In the configuration graph, the move subsequence is a walk of five moves:
        it starts at <m>(0,0,0,0)</m>, visits <m>(1,1,2,3)</m>, and ends at the
        <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref>. The repeated vertices record
        the checks, not additional moves.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-reconstructing-trajectories-from-logs">
    <title>Reconstructing Trajectories from Logs</title>

    <p>
      The raw log does not record the full state after every event. Move rows
      record only the moved card and its destination zone. Check rows record the
      full checked configuration. Reset rows record a return to the initial
      state. Reconstructing a trajectory means combining these row types into a
      coherent state sequence.
    </p>

    <p>
      Reconstruction begins with the initial state <m>X_0</m> and processes the
      log in time order, using <c>Problem View</c> or timestamp order to break
      ties when needed. Let <m>\widehat{X}</m> denote the current reconstructed
      state and <m>\widehat{s}</m> its corresponding configuration function.
    </p>

    <p>
      For each move row, the payload identifies a card <m>j</m> and a destination
      zone <m>h</m>. The source zone <m>i</m> is inferred from the current
      reconstructed state:
      <md>
        i=\widehat{s}(j).
      </md>
      The replay update is
      <md>
        \widehat{X}\leftarrow \widehat{X}+(e_h-e_i)f_j^{\top}.
      </md>
      Real logs occasionally contain a drop whose destination equals the card's
      current zone, so that <m>h=i</m>. The model's moves require
      <m>h\neq i</m>, but the replay rule tolerates such rows harmlessly because
      the update is then zero. Whether to retain, flag, or omit these no-op drops
      is a processing decision.
    </p>

    <p>
      For each reset row, the current state is replaced by the initial state:
      <md>
        \widehat{X}\leftarrow X_0.
      </md>
      The reset is retained as a distinguished event in the trajectory.
    </p>

    <p>
      For each check row, replay does not change the current reconstructed state.
      Instead, the check row is parsed into an independently observed checked
      state, which the next section compares with the replayed state.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-trajectory-reconstruction-rules">
      <title>State reconstruction rules by event type.</title>
      <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
        <col width="25%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="35%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="40%" halign="left"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Event type</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Payload</p></cell>
          <cell><p>State update</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>move</p></cell>
          <cell><p>card <m>j</m> and destination zone <m>h</m></p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\widehat{X}\leftarrow \widehat{X}+(e_h-e_i)f_j^{\top}</m>, where <m>i=\widehat{s}(j)</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>check</p></cell>
          <cell><p>checked configuration JSON and feedback</p></cell>
          <cell><p>No replay update; mark current state and compare it with the parsed checked state.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>reset</p></cell>
          <cell><p>reset marker</p></cell>
          <cell><p><m>\widehat{X}\leftarrow X_0</m></p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>page</p></cell>
          <cell><p>page-view marker</p></cell>
          <cell><p>No board-state update; may delimit a session.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The output of reconstruction is an analysis-ready trajectory: a state
      sequence, action labels, timestamps, check marks, reset marks, and any page
      or session delimiters needed for later feature extraction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-check-anchors-and-consistency-checks">
    <title>Check Anchors</title>

    <p>
      Check rows provide an independent record of the board state at particular
      moments, so checked states double as <term>anchors</term> for validating the
      replayed trajectory.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>k\in K</m> be a check index. Let <m>\widehat{X}_k</m> be the state
      reconstructed by replaying moves and resets up to that check, and let
      <m>X_k^{\operatorname{check}}</m> be the state parsed from the check JSON.
      The basic consistency condition is
      <md xml:id="an-check-anchor-consistency">
        \widehat{X}_k=X_k^{\operatorname{check}}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Because the tray is implicit in the check JSON, parsing
      <m>X_k^{\operatorname{check}}</m> requires the full card roster. If a card
      is absent from every listed <xref ref="gt-slot" text="custom">slot</xref>,
      it is assigned to the tray:
      <md xml:id="an-implicit-tray-parsing">
        s_k^{\operatorname{check}}(j)=0
        \qquad
        \text{if card }j\text{ does not appear in any checked slot list.}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      When the anchor condition holds at every check, the replayed trajectory
      agrees with the platform's own record of the board. When it fails, the
      trajectory is unreliable between anchors and must be flagged before feature
      extraction. Diagnosing mismatch causes and managing quality-control flags
      belongs to the data-processing chapter; here the anchor condition itself is
      the point. The trajectory model comes with an internal consistency test.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-transition-to-features">
    <title>From Trajectories to Features</title>

    <p>
      This chapter closes the loop opened by the state model. There, distance was
      a property of pairs of configurations. Here, adjacency in the configuration
      graph makes one unit of distance one single-card move, so an attempt becomes
      a measurable walk through state space.
    </p>

    <p>
      The event trajectory <m>\Gamma</m> is the object the rest of the framework
      consumes. Outcome features look at checked states and compare them with the
      key. Process features use the order, actions, and timing of the whole
      trajectory. Cohort features aggregate both across students. The next chapter
      begins with the outcome layer before returning to the trajectory for process
      summaries.
    </p>

    <p>
      Throughout, this chapter has avoided claims about student reasoning. A
      trajectory records how the board changed over time; what those changes mean
      depends on the <xref ref="gt-item-design" text="custom">item design</xref>,
      the feedback policy, the reconstruction quality, and the validation
      questions taken up in later chapters.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
